item+ function src/lattice.rs :: pick_state_path :: pub fn pick_state_path(facts: StateFacts, system: &std::path::Path, user: Option<&std::path::Path>) -> PathBuf { :: The resolution order (v1.4.1), stated exactly as shipped: 1. QEXO_STATE, when set non-empty (the caller's word). 2. THE WRITE-INTENT RULE (new in v1.4.1): a write-intending verb in unprivileged hands, facing a system state it cannot write, selects the per-user path instead — existing or to be born there by config. The system state belongs to its owner; this user's node is their own. (Read-only verbs are untouched by this rule and keep the order below exactly as v1.4.0 shipped it.) 2b. THE LEARN RULE (new in v1.4.2): `qexo learn` in unprivileged hands, when a per-user state EXISTS, selects the per-user path — the deck belongs to the operator, not to the machine's owner. With no per-user state, learn falls through to the order below like any reader (v1.4.1 diverted it to the missing path and errored; v1.4.0 answered). 3. The system path, when the system state exists and this process can read it — a system install stays canonical, byte-for-byte, and read-only verbs never fail on its permissions when a per-user state exists instead (rule 4 is what they fall to). 4. The per-user path, when a per-user state exists. 5. The system path again when it exists but cannot be read and no per-user state exists — the honest error names the real file rather than inventing an empty node. 6. Nothing exists: the system path for a privileged process, the per-user path otherwise (a fresh `qexo config` as a normal user births a per-user node); with no home directory to anchor to, the system path. A node has ONE state: the resolution finds the first that exists, and `qexo status` names which path is live. :: doc comment :: 771 :: 1
item+ function src/lattice.rs :: prefix_scrolls :: pub fn prefix_scrolls<'a>(px: &'a Phext, prefix: &[u32]) -> Vec<(Coord, &'a String)> { :: Every scroll whose address starts with `prefix`, in ascending (coordinate) order — the range read of a run or a homeland. :: doc comment :: 172 :: 1
