item+ function src/lattice.rs :: regen_indexes :: pub fn regen_indexes(px: &mut Phext) { :: Regenerate the home index and every homeland index scroll from the records present. Called by config at birth, by commands after every mutation, and by `doctor --fix` as its repair. :: doc comment :: 352 :: 1
item+ function src/lattice.rs :: save :: pub fn save(&mut self) -> Result<(), String> { :: Persist the whole document. Phext serializes canonically (ascending coordinates, delimiter ladder), so a save is a rewrite of one file — the format's write model, accepted.  Every save first runs the state top-up (v1.4.1): `seed_missing_indexes` makes the document whole under the running binary's Layer 2 layout before it is written. save() is the write boundary — every mutating verb ends here, and no read or --check path ever reaches it (their byte-inert guarantees are untouched) — so an inherited state gains the homelands its birth binary predates on the first write the new binary makes, whichever verb that write belongs to. :: doc comment :: 956 :: 1
item+ function src/lattice.rs :: seed_missing_indexes :: pub fn seed_missing_indexes(px: &mut Phext) -> Vec<Coord> { :: The state top-up (v1.4.1): seed any index scroll the current layout defines that this document lacks, with exactly the text `regen_indexes` would give it. Born of the v1.4.0 fleet refusal: states born under 1.3.0 carry no lessons homeland, so the new layout's index is missing through no damage of theirs — an upgraded state becomes whole by the running binary's own hand the first time it writes. Surgical by construction: an index that EXISTS is never touched (records and indexes alike), a missing one is copied from a scratch regeneration of this same document. Idempotent — a whole state seeds nothing. Returns the addresses seeded. :: doc comment :: 630 :: 1
item+ function src/lattice.rs :: set_field :: pub fn set_field(text: &str, key: &str, value: &str) -> String { :: Set `key` in a record scroll: replace its line if present, append it if not. Edits the scroll in place, as Layer 2 asks. :: doc comment :: 249 :: 1
