item+ function src/plan.rs :: compute :: pub fn compute(tasks: &[TaskInput], current: &DiskObs, history: &[DiskObs], host: (f64, f64), gate: &GateInputs) -> PlanData { :: Compute the plan: - byte gate: the worse of the lenses that are ON (see evaluate_gate) — the volume lens judges the measured volume's free fraction; the quota lens, only where a node configures it, reproduces the old absolute ceiling (AMBER 95 GB / RED 102 GB, thresholds anchored to the 2026-11-08 EDQUOT failure at ~106 GB on the Muse VM's staging quota — a real ceiling on that one machine, a false verdict everywhere else). - file gate (v2.2): AMBER/RED at the configured counts under the named staging root — the count is the faster-moving half of the disk currency, and a byte-only gate can sit GREEN while the count runs at the wall. - disk gate = the worse of the two halves. - forecast gate (v2.3, re-based on the lenses in v1.0.3): a GREEN disk gate escalates to AMBER when the nearest crossing projected under the node's configured lenses is within 3 days. A planner that only watches crossings gives no lead time — and a forecast that projects levels the gate no longer reads gives false alarms instead. :: doc comment :: 646 :: 1
item+ function src/plan.rs :: days_from_civil :: pub fn days_from_civil(y: i64, m: i64, d: i64) -> i64 { :: Days from civil date (Howard Hinnant's algorithm) — the v2.3 date arithmetic, carried verbatim in behavior. :: doc comment :: 714 :: 1
item+ function src/plan.rs :: forecast_gate :: pub fn forecast_gate(history: &[DiskObs], cfg: &GateConfig, total_bytes: Option<u64>) -> Forecast { :: The forecast computation (v2.3 semantics, re-based on the gate's lenses in v1.0.3; rates re-based on the median interval in v1.2.1): rate growth from the history — the median of the per-interval rates over the most recent FORECAST_WINDOW_INTERVALS intervals, falling back to the single interval when the history holds only two observations — then project days to the crossings of the SAME thresholds evaluate_gate reads — the volume lens's free fractions of this volume's total, the quota lens's ceilings (only when the node has that lens on), and the file lens's configured counts. Until v1.0.3 the byte half projected the legacy absolute levels (95/102 GB) whatever the node's configuration, so the forecast contradicted the gate it forecasts on every ranch volume.  A crossing the current reading has already reached is the gate's verdict, not a forecast: projecting it would report 0.0 days for a level the lenses already own — exactly the v1.0.2 fleet failure, where the second plan on a healthy ranch node flipped to FORECAST AMBER because its volume had long since passed a ceiling that no longer applies to it. Flat or falling growth projects nothing, and nothing is projected beyond FORECAST_HORIZON_DAYS. Fewer than two usable observations degrades to unavailable — never invented.  Why the median (v1.2.1, from the 2026-11-12 evaluation of 2026-11-11 and re-proven by 2026-11-12's interval): rating from the last interval alone made the nearest-crossing estimate whipsaw with whatever the last day happened to do — file AMBER read ~1.4 days out on the 2026-11-10 interval, ~14 days out on 2026-11-11's, ~9.9 days out on 2026-11-12's, while the underlying series never changed character. The median of the recent intervals reports the series' typical rate: a planner whose warnings hold still long enough to act on. :: doc comment :: 491 :: 1
