item+ struct src/dispatch.rs :: Plan :: pub struct Plan { :: What a legal transition will do, before anything is written: the state it lands in (a third consecutive failure lands in `paused-auto`, not `queued`, §8), the counters after it, the register cite it sets, and the audit it owes (§7.2), if any. :: doc comment :: 692 :: 1
item+ struct src/dispatch.rs :: RunLines :: pub struct RunLines { :: One verb run's rendered lines, split by stream, plus the structured facts a second driver renders in its own shape (slice 7, spec §2.3). A verb core fills a RunLines and never prints directly: in `live` mode each line is also printed as it is produced — the CLI driver's mode, byte- and order-identical to the verb's historic printing — while the `--app` driver captures the run and renders `facts` as its `key: value` block instead. The core is one; the drivers can never disagree about what a verb did, only about how it is rendered. :: doc comment :: 54 :: 1
item+ struct src/dispatch.rs :: Transition :: pub struct Transition { :: A transition request (spec §4.6). `evidence` is the note, the failure evidence, or the resolution evidence, per the transition; `checked`/`showed`/`verdict` are the F4 live-surface evidence shape (§7) a `done` requires; `register` supplies the register cite a move to `waiting` needs when the record does not already carry one; `manual` distinguishes a human resume from any automatic one — `paused-auto` yields only to the manual kind (§8). :: doc comment :: 653 :: 1
item+ struct src/dispatch.rs :: TransitionReceipt :: pub struct TransitionReceipt { :: A transition receipt (6.6.6, Collection 2, action: transition), parsed — the doctor's evidence (§4.5). :: doc comment :: 1058 :: 1
item+ function src/doctor.rs :: check_queue :: pub fn check_queue(lat: &Lattice) -> Report { :: Doctor's queue section. The §4.5 counts: items by state; terminal items lacking a receipt (must be 0); receipts citing no live item (must be 0); assignments whose heartbeat is stale (counted here; since slice 5 the F5 path acts on them — a stale assignment for an item homed here with no detection on file is a finding, and the failure counter is checked against its receipted history, §8); an audit record for every non-ping closure (a closure without one is a finding, §7.2 — a register-close audit satisfies it for a register-driven closure, §10.3). Slice 3 adds the register link's integrity: a register-born item with no register-seq, a register-seq carried by two items, and the pending write-back count (visible, never a failure — the drain's sweep retries them, §10.4). The §4.6 illegal combinations are checked one by one, each in its own words. No queue finding is fixable by doctor: the repair is a transition or Will's hand, never an invention — a nonzero must-be-0 count is a doctor failure of the same class as a missing homeland index (§4.5). :: doc comment :: 485 :: 1
item+ function src/doctor.rs :: fix_lattice :: pub fn fix_lattice(lat: &mut Lattice) -> Vec<String> { :: Repair the lattice layer: regenerate every index scroll from the records. Returns the human list of what changed. :: doc comment :: 407 :: 1
