item+ function src/wire.rs :: build_bundle_parts :: pub fn build_bundle_parts( px: &Phext, item: &Item, attempt: u32, resume_from: Option<&str>, resume_text: Option<&str>, bridge: Option<(&str, &str)>, ) -> Result<(Vec<Phext>, String), String> { :: Build a bundle's universes (unsigned) and manifest for `item` as it stands in `px` — the record pre-transition (state queued, attempts = attempt−1). `resume_text` is the checkpoint scroll the origin holds in custody for `resume_from`'s cite (§8): when both are present, the checkpoint rides as the bundle's last universe, provenanced like any context universe, so the replacement executor receives the resume currency itself — not just its address. :: doc comment :: 266 :: 1
item+ function src/wire.rs :: check_checkpoint_outputs :: pub fn check_checkpoint_outputs(text: &str, read: &dyn Fn(&str) -> Option<String>) -> Result<usize, String> { :: Verify a checkpoint's cited outputs (§8): every `output:` line names a scroll, every `output-sha256:` line the hash the executor took of it at checkpoint time; `read` fetches a cited scroll's current text (the origin reads over the scroll route). Ok(n) = n outputs read back and hash-matched. A checkpoint citing no outputs returns Ok(0) — it verifies nothing and proves no progress; the caller treats only n ≥ 1 as a verified progress event. Err names the first failure: an unreadable output, a hash that does not match, or a cite/hash count mismatch. :: doc comment :: 2070 :: 1
item+ function src/wire.rs :: cmd_checkpoint :: pub fn cmd_checkpoint(args: &[String]) -> i32 { :: `qexo checkpoint <item-address> --note <text> [--output <coordinate>]…` — executor-local (§6): a checkpoint scroll in the attempt universe's item Book (the next free scroll at Scroll 4+), a running → running oracle transition, and the cite recorded on the attempt copy and the assignment. Each `--output` names a scroll this attempt produced (a bare coordinate, or `$<n>:<coordinate>` to name its universe); the executor resolves it from its own chain and records the scroll's SHA-256 beside the cite — the pair the origin later reads back to verify progress (§8). An output that cannot be read here is refused before anything is written: a checkpoint never cites what its own node cannot produce. :: doc comment :: 2583 :: 1
item+ function src/wire.rs :: cmd_dispatch :: pub fn cmd_dispatch(item_arg: Option<&str>, to: Option<&str>, check: bool) -> i32 { :: `qexo dispatch [<item-address>] [--to <node>] [--check]` — §2.1, §5, §6. One save, at the end, on success; --check writes nothing at all. The CLI driver: the run below, printed live, byte for byte as always. :: doc comment :: 1418 :: 1
item+ function src/wire.rs :: cmd_fail :: pub fn cmd_fail(args: &[String]) -> i32 { :: `qexo fail <item-address> --note <evidence>` — executor-local (§4.6): the oracle's failure transition on the attempt copy with the evidence the spec requires (the plan lands the third consecutive failure in paused-auto, §8), and the assignment retired with the same evidence as its status — the origin reads it on its next heartbeat reconciliation. :: doc comment :: 2734 :: 1
item+ function src/wire.rs :: cmd_heartbeat :: pub fn cmd_heartbeat(item_arg: &str) -> i32 { :: `qexo heartbeat <item-address>` — one verb, two sides (§6). Executor side: beat — the `heartbeat:` line lands on the assignment record and the attempt universe's record scroll; the first beat moves the item dispatched → running through the oracle. Origin side: read the executor's assignment record over the scroll route and reconcile the home record with what it says — or, on §8's evidences, detect the executor's loss and teleport the item. :: doc comment :: 2196 :: 1
